  "worked_solution": "This time, we'll use $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ notation. First, we compute the derivative, which gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(5{x}^{4}+\\sqrt[3]{{x}^{2}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(5{x}^{4}+{x}^{2/3}) \\\\  & =4\\cdot 5{x}^{3}+\\frac{2}{3}\\cdot {x}^{-1/3} \\\\  & =20{x}^{3}+\\frac{2{x}^{-1/3}}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating at $x=1$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{x=1} & =20(1{)}^{3}+\\frac{2(1{)}^{-1/3}}{3} \\\\  & =20+\\frac{2}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{62}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the tangent to the curve is $\\frac{62}{3}.$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
